Mapping one Gaussian distribution to another can always be done by scaling the width and normalization constant, and this is what the corrections in Table 3 do. Since these distributions can be fitted with Gaussians, it makes sense that the corrections work so well.

It is easy to extract \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) from PYTHIA no-GEANT, p+p, and d+Au. For the 6-7 GeV range, \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) is \(\ 630\pm 12\pm 30\) MeV/c for d+Au, and \(\ 690\pm 12\pm 60\) MeV/c for p+p. For the 15-20 GeV range, \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) is \(\ 630\pm 13\pm 30\) MeV/c for d+Au, and \(\ 573\pm 9\pm 30\) MeV/c for p+p. These averages are dependent on the scale factors of Table 3. The systematic uncertainties of these \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) values come from uncertainties in the parameters of Table 3. In all cases, the data do not significantly differ from the PYTHIA no-GEANT-simulated \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) value of 627 MeV/c. The PYTHIA value is statistically very precise, and does not increase with jet \(E_{T}\) between 6 and 20 GeV. These values show that for p+p, the \(\sqrt{\left\langle j_{T}^{\ 2}\right\rangle}\) differs by less than two sigma between 6 to 7 and 15 to 20 GeV jets, and for d+Au jets, there are no differences between energy ranges. Assuming that the differences between the two p+p measurements are due to the sample size, it seems plausible to take the average of the two p+p measurements weighted by systematic uncertainty: \(\ 612\pm 9\pm 30\) MeV/c.

